WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.164 --> 00:00:06.766
FAYE MOSS: Hello again! I'm Faye Moss, and welcome to Superstars in

2
00:00:06.767 --> 00:00:13.809
History , the only talk show that brings you exclusive interviews with fabulous famous people from the

3
00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:18.088
past. I know you'll be as thrilled about today's guest as I

4
00:00:18.089 --> 00:00:27.826
am. He was a pilot and a spy during World War II, but you probably know him for his wacky and enormously entertaining children's books. Boys

5
00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:54.742
and girls, meet the author of James and the Giant Peach , Charlie and the Chocolate Factory , Matilda , and more . . . Roald

6
00:00:54.743 --> 00:00:55.970
Dahl!

7
00:00:55.971 --> 00:01:01.787
ROALD DAHL: Hello! FAYE MOSS: Hi, Roald. Thanks so much for being here today

8
00:01:01.788 --> 00:01:06.350
on Superstars in History . Your books are so famous, they're like

9
00:01:06.351 --> 00:01:11.130
required reading for kids. Maybe that's because they feature kid

10
00:01:11.131 --> 00:01:15.382
characters in challenging situations. How much of your writing was inspired by

11
00:01:15.383 --> 00:01:24.987
your own childhood? ROALD DAHL: That's quite a difficult question to answer, Faye. I was born in Wales in 1916, the

12
00:01:24.988 --> 00:01:32.957
third of five children. When I was three years old, my oldest sister suddenly died, and weeks later, my

13
00:01:32.958 --> 00:01:43.848
father died too. My mother considered moving the family to Norway, where my parents had been born, but she ultimately decided to stay in Wales and move later to

14
00:01:43.849 --> 00:01:51.985
England. You see, my father believed English schools were the best in the world and he wanted his children to be educated there.

15
00:01:51.986 --> 00:01:57.748
FAYE MOSS: In your novel Matilda , you spend a lot of time focusing on Matilda's

16
00:01:57.749 --> 00:02:04.074
school life. There's even a terrifying headmistress famous for her violent and creative

17
00:02:04.075 --> 00:02:07.483
punishments. Did you draw on real-life experiences for

18
00:02:07.484 --> 00:02:11.692
this? ROALD DAHL: Well, I do remember my school days

19
00:02:11.693 --> 00:02:16.466
quite vividly. Back then, punishments were very harsh, and they

20
00:02:16.467 --> 00:02:20.154
often hurt. My last boarding school was particularly

21
00:02:20.155 --> 00:02:26.883
violent. The teachers were brutal, and older students were allowed to beat and bully younger ones.

22
00:02:26.884 --> 00:02:34.566
FAYE MOSS: Oh my. I can't even imagine. You must have terrible memories from your

23
00:02:34.567 --> 00:02:43.479
school days! ROALD DAHL: It wasn't all bad. The great chocolate manufacturer, Cadbury, sent samples of chocolate to students at one of

24
00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:47.707
my schools. Then we'd fill out a form to let them know how we

25
00:02:47.708 --> 00:02:58.169
liked them. I used to dream of making a marvelous chocolate bar that would impress Mr. Cadbury

26
00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:04.262
himself. FAYE MOSS: Hmm . . . that sounds like something that may have inspired one of

27
00:03:04.263 --> 00:03:08.389
your books! Did you know early on that you wanted to be a

28
00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:13.379
writer? ROALD DAHL: Not at all—in fact my teachers thought I was a

29
00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:21.649
terrible writer. After high school, I didn't attend university, and in 1934, I started working for an oil

30
00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:28.016
company. A few years later, they dispatched me to Africa, and I was there when World War II began.

31
00:03:28.017 --> 00:03:38.750
I joined the Royal Air Force and became a pilot, but before I even saw combat, I crashed my plane in the desert. FAYE

32
00:03:38.751 --> 00:03:41.369
MOSS: Luckily you

33
00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:47.530
survived! ROALD DAHL: Indeed! But my injuries were serious, and I spent a long time in

34
00:03:47.531 --> 00:03:55.380
a hospital. It took six months before I was declared fit to fly again, and my career as a pilot ended up quite brief.

35
00:03:55.381 --> 00:04:01.927
The injuries from my crash still affected me, so the RAF sent me back to England.

36
00:04:01.928 --> 00:04:07.644
FAYE MOSS: You were only 24, and already you'd had such an exciting

37
00:04:07.645 --> 00:04:10.731
life! You weren't even a famous author yet.

38
00:04:10.732 --> 00:04:20.000
ROALD DAHL: We're getting to that part. In 1942 I was sent to work in Washington D.C. at the British

39
00:04:20.001 --> 00:04:25.585
Embassy. Another British government worker was writing an article about the war for a magazine.

40
00:04:25.586 --> 00:04:33.531
He asked me for some recollections of my war experience, so I wrote down an account of my plane crash for him. Once

41
00:04:33.532 --> 00:04:42.767
he read my story, he decided to submit it, without making any changes! This inspired me to write more stories. FAYE MOSS:

42
00:04:42.768 --> 00:04:48.063
Many of your early stories were about your short career as a pilot, right? ROALD

43
00:04:48.064 --> 00:04:53.487
DAHL: Right. Walt Disney even wanted to make a movie out of one of my stories.

44
00:04:53.488 --> 00:05:01.749
The movie never got made, but it was adapted into a children's book that became popular with Eleanor Roosevelt. FAYE

45
00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:07.852
MOSS: Walt Disney! Eleanor Roosevelt! Wow, you knew a lot of famous people.

46
00:05:07.853 --> 00:05:12.522
I've heard rumors that you even spied on President Roosevelt!

47
00:05:12.523 --> 00:05:19.645
ROALD DAHL: I guess you could say I was a spy, but in truth, I was a very boring

48
00:05:19.646 --> 00:05:28.091
spy. The prime minister of the United Kingdom, Winston Churchill, wanted to stay informed about President Roosevelt's thoughts and feelings.

49
00:05:28.092 --> 00:05:32.091
My job was to pass on any such information. Not

50
00:05:32.092 --> 00:05:43.240
very exciting, you see! After the war, I married Patricia Neal, a Hollywood actress, started a family, and continued to write. FAYE MOSS:

51
00:05:43.241 --> 00:05:50.835
It wasn't until 1960 that you started writing specifically for children. Why the sudden change? ROALD

52
00:05:50.836 --> 00:05:58.183
DAHL: By 1960 I had run out of ideas for stories, and my publisher was becoming impatient.

53
00:05:58.184 --> 00:06:02.083
Meanwhile I'd been making up stories for my own children. I

54
00:06:02.084 --> 00:06:09.530
wrote one of them down and sent it to my publisher, and eventually it became James and the Giant Peach . By

55
00:06:09.531 --> 00:06:15.733
the end of the year, I had finished the first draft of Charlie and the Chocolate Factory . FAYE

56
00:06:15.734 --> 00:06:25.162
MOSS: You achieved professional success in the 1960s, but they were a very hard time for you personally, weren't they?

57
00:06:25.163 --> 00:06:31.133
ROALD DAHL: Yes, the 1960s were a time of great personal

58
00:06:31.134 --> 00:06:39.112
difficulty. My infant son was in a serious accident in 1960. He survived but was plagued with health issues.

59
00:06:39.113 --> 00:06:43.814
In 1962 my oldest daughter died of measles.

60
00:06:43.815 --> 00:06:52.922
And in 1965 my wife suffered a series of strokes, and she had to learn to walk and talk all over again. FAYE

61
00:06:52.923 --> 00:06:56.300
MOSS: That sounds really challenging.

62
00:06:56.301 --> 00:07:02.924
ROALD DAHL: It was, and through all of it, I continued writing and working on film and TV

63
00:07:02.925 --> 00:07:09.884
projects. Did you know that I wrote the original script for the movie Willy Wonka and the Chocolate Factory

64
00:07:09.885 --> 00:07:21.592
? FAYE MOSS: Yes, I did. In total, you wrote 34 books that have been translated into 58 languages and have sold over 250

65
00:07:21.593 --> 00:07:24.266
million copies. That's quite a

66
00:07:24.267 --> 00:07:29.637
remarkable career! Thank you for joining me today, Roald. We'll let you get back to history

67
00:07:29.638 --> 00:07:35.820
now. ROALD

68
00:07:35.821 --> 00:07:40.589
DAHL: Good-bye, Faye. FAYE MOSS: Thanks, everyone, for tuning in for my

69
00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:49.352
interview with Roald Dahl. He continued his fantastic career until November 23, 1990, when he died at the

70
00:07:49.353 --> 00:07:57.851
age of 74. He left behind a large body of work that still brings joy and a love of reading to people all over

71
00:07:57.852 --> 00:08:04.578
the world. Our time is up for today, but I'll be back again soon with a new special guest from the past.

72
00:08:04.579 --> 00:08:14.720
You've been watching Faye Moss. Good-bye!

